---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'; import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro'; import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro'; import CTASection from '@/components/CTASection.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why Portal Page exists: to give every meaningful community a private digital place it can call its own.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
  <PageHero eyebrow="About Portal Page" title="The internet gave everyone a profile. Communities deserve a place of their own." intro="Portal Page exists to give organisations and their people a direct, trusted digital relationship—without an advertising platform in the middle." tone="lime" />
  <section class="content-section"><h2>From places to profiles to feeds.</h2><div class="content-prose"><p>Early online communities often gathered around a place: a forum, a bulletin board, a shared topic. The experience could be awkward, but the destination was understandable. People visited because they cared about the subject and recognised the group around it.</p><p>Social networks shifted the centre of gravity. Each person received a profile, and communities became items inside a personalised feed. That made discovery and participation easier at enormous scale. It also placed community relationships inside business models built around attention, advertising and behavioural prediction.</p><p>Organisations gained reach but gave up control. An important update could compete with entertainment and sponsored content. Community identity could be shaped by recommendation systems. Member activity could contribute to profiles that followed people elsewhere. The organisation's relationship with its own people became dependent on the rules of another platform.</p><p>Email, messaging and workplace tools filled some gaps, but often created a different problem: fragmentation. The community now exists in six places, with no reliable front door and no shared memory.</p></div></section>
  <section class="content-section"><h2>Audiences are not communities.</h2><div class="content-prose"><p>An audience receives. A community participates. Followers recognise a publisher. Members recognise a relationship with one another and with the organisation that convenes them.</p><p>That does not mean every member needs to speak every day. Many healthy communities are quiet until something useful happens. A resident finds the moving guide. A volunteer checks the event time. A professional reads a considered answer. A program participant sees an official update. The infrastructure succeeds by supporting purpose, not by producing endless activity.</p><p>Portal Page distinguishes reach from belonging, attention from participation and platform ownership from community control. Organisations should be able to define their membership, structure, standards and information environment. Members should be able to understand where they are, who operates the space and what happens to their information.</p><aside class="callout"><strong>Our mission</strong>To give every meaningful community a digital place it can call its own.</aside></div></section>
  <section class="content-section"><h2>A company shaped by the product principle.</h2><div class="content-prose"><p>Portal Page intends to build a commercial model in which organisations pay for useful community software. It should not sell personal information, build advertising profiles from community behaviour or operate a behavioural advertising marketplace. Private community content should not automatically become artificial intelligence training material.</p><p>The company should practise the same restraint in its public claims. No founders, employees, offices, funding, customers, contracts, certifications or awards are listed because verified details have not been supplied. The <a href="/team/">team page</a> is ready for real profiles, and the <a href="/communities/">communities page</a> is ready for consenting customer stories when they exist.</p><p>Portal Page's public principles are an intended standard. The work is to make the product architecture, business operations, customer agreements and everyday decisions live up to them.</p></div></section>
  <section class="statement"><p class="eyebrow">The remembered idea</p><h2>Your community. Your platform. Your data.</h2><p>Not another social network. Private digital infrastructure for real communities.</p></section>
  <CTASection />
</BaseLayout>
